ux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186554629 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186557238 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=3,$ $y=-1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186557290 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=x\\sqrt{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186557319 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and the surfaces $x=y-z$ and $x=1+{y}^{2}.$ Given th